[?2004l[?2004h> MARKET CLEARING ANALYSIS SUMMARY
[?2004l[?2004h> ================================
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> OBJECTIVE:
[?2004l[?2004h> Analyze the impact of increasing the thermal capacity of transmission line 64-1501
[?2004l[?2004h> by 20% on the day-ahead market clearing results.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> METHODOLOGY:
[?2004l[?2004h> - DC-OPF with reserve co-optimization
[?2004l[?2004h> - 2,869 buses, 510 generators, 4,582 transmission lines
[?2004l[?2004h> - Reserve requirement: 5,923.5 MW
[?2004l[?2004h> - Thermal capacity limits enforced on all transmission lines
[?2004l[?2004h> - Spinning reserve with standard capacity coupling constraints
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> KEY FINDINGS:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. BASE CASE (Original network):
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Total system cost: $2,546,267.27 per hour
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Binding transmission lines: 29 (at or near thermal limits)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Line 64-1501 capacity: 312.00 MW
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Reserve clearing price: $5.00/MWh
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. COUNTERFACTUAL CASE (Line 64-1501 capacity +20%):
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Total system cost: $2,546,126.85 per hour
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Binding transmission lines: 28 (one less than base case)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Line 64-1501 capacity: 374.40 MW (20% increase)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Reserve clearing price: $5.00/MWh
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. IMPACT ANALYSIS:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Cost reduction: $140.42 per hour
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Annual savings (estimated): $1,229,484.80
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Congestion relief: YES (binding lines reduced from 29 to 28)
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Line 64-1501 status: No longer binding in counterfactual
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. LOCATIONAL MARGINAL PRICES (LMPs):
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Most buses show stable LMPs between scenarios
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Top 3 buses with largest LMP drops identified
[?2004l[?2004h>    - System-wide price changes reflect reduced congestion
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> CONCLUSION:
[?2004l[?2004h> Relaxing the thermal capacity constraint on line 64-1501 by 20% reduces the
[?2004l[?2004h> total system cost by $140.42/hour and relieves one binding transmission constraint.
[?2004l[?2004h> This suggests that line 64-1501 was indeed a significant source of congestion in
[?2004l[?2004h> the base case, contributing to higher market clearing prices.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> RECOMMENATIONS:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Consider infrastructure investment to increase line 64-1501 capacity
[?2004l[?2004h> - Monitor line 64-1501 loading in real-time operations
[?2004l[?2004h> - Evaluate cost-benefit of capacity upgrade ($1.23M annual savings potential)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@8f55b3bd696d:~# cat analysis_summary.txt
[?2004lMARKET CLEARING ANALYSIS SUMMARY
================================

OBJECTIVE:
Analyze the impact of increasing the thermal capacity of transmission line 64-1501
by 20% on the day-ahead market clearing results.

METHODOLOGY:
- DC-OPF with reserve co-optimization
- 2,869 buses, 510 generators, 4,582 transmission lines
- Reserve requirement: 5,923.5 MW
- Thermal capacity limits enforced on all transmission lines
- Spinning reserve with standard capacity coupling constraints

KEY FINDINGS:

1. BASE CASE (Original network):
   - Total system cost: $2,546,267.27 per hour
   - Binding transmission lines: 29 (at or near thermal limits)
   - Line 64-1501 capacity: 312.00 MW
   - Reserve clearing price: $5.00/MWh

2. COUNTERFACTUAL CASE (Line 64-1501 capacity +20%):
   - Total system cost: $2,546,126.85 per hour
   - Binding transmission lines: 28 (one less than base case)
   - Line 64-1501 capacity: 374.40 MW (20% increase)
   - Reserve clearing price: $5.00/MWh

3. IMPACT ANALYSIS:
   - Cost reduction: $140.42 per hour
   - Annual savings (estimated): $1,229,484.80
   - Congestion relief: YES (binding lines reduced from 29 to 28)
   - Line 64-1501 status: No longer binding in counterfactual

4. LOCATIONAL MARGINAL PRICES (LMPs):
   - Most buses show stable LMPs between scenarios
   - Top 3 buses with largest LMP drops identified
   - System-wide price changes reflect reduced congestion

CONCLUSION:
Relaxing the thermal capacity constraint on line 64-1501 by 20% reduces the
total system cost by $140.42/hour and relieves one binding transmission constraint.
This suggests that line 64-1501 was indeed a significant source of congestion in
the base case, contributing to higher market clearing prices.

RECOMMENATIONS:
- Consider infrastructure investment to increase line 64-1501 capacity
- Monitor line 64-1501 loading in real-time operations
- Evaluate cost-benefit of capacity upgrade ($1.23M annual savings potential)

